WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:09.120
After the 2016 election, some school
choice advocates hoped for a huge federal

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:12.599
choice initiative. But the last two
decades of federal K-12 policy

00:00:12.599 --> 00:00:17.130
demonstrates why that might not be so
wise. Big, bold Washington reforms can

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:21.000
misunderstand the important differences
between communities; generate bulky,

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:26.010
clumsy programs; and as a result, engender
resentment among local stakeholders. So

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:29.580
choice advocates might thank Uncle Sam
for his interest, but ask him to step

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:34.500
back. For 25 years, states and locals have
led on school choice, and to great effect!

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:38.520
Today, three million children attend
charters, and another half million are using

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:41.790
the various private school choice
programs. This has been built on a

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:47.100
humbler approach to policymaking than
swift, certain DC initiatives. It elevates

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:51.480
parents, local nonprofits, and state
leaders – those who know their communities

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:55.289
inside and out, and who will have to live
with the consequences of their decisions.

00:00:55.289 --> 00:00:58.980
Yes, they might move more slowly at first
and make course corrections along the

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:03.449
way, but the result can be smart, tailored
programs that have deep local support. We

00:01:03.449 --> 00:01:07.440
should continue to trust federalism and
localism when it comes to school choice.

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:11.540
What do you think about Uncle Sam's role
in school choice? Let us know in your

00:01:11.549 --> 00:01:16.200
comments, and to learn more, read my
article in the AEI publication "This Way

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:20.670
Up." Also, let us know what other topics
you'd like our scholars to cover in 60

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:25.700
seconds, and be sure to like and
subscribe for more research and videos from AEI.

